87644}, {"time": "2026-07-28 10:00:00", "open": 4072.69503, "high": 4073.30747, "low": 4058.44156, "close": 4059.05246}, {"time": "2026-07-28 11:00:00", "open": 4059.84612, "high": 4059.92449, "low": 4042.49891, "close": 4045.08006}, {"time": "2026-07-28 12:00:00", "open": 4044.87602, "high": 4049.06795, "low": 4040.73056, "close": 4043.42602}, {"time": "2026-07-28 13:00:00", "open": 4042.92783, "high": 4050.87904, "low": 4041.05636, "close": 4049.39138}], "calendar": [], "editorial": "Gold is trading at $4,049 as the European session gives way to the early New York window, sitting roughly $125 below the mid-July peak near $4,202 and pressing against a cluster of daily lows that have defined the recent consolidation range. The daily chart tells a story of a market that ran hard into resistance and has since been unable to reclaim any meaningful ground, with each attempted recovery fading before it can threaten the highs.\n\nThe structural story begins with the sharp rally that carried gold from the $3,950 area in late June all the way to $4,202 by early July \u2014 a move of roughly 250 points in under a fortnight. That prior reaction zone around $4,207 has now registered four touches and has consistently rejected price, suggesting the market has found genuine supply in that region. Since that rejection, the daily candles have printed a sequence of lower closes, with the 13 July session delivering a particularly aggressive sell-off from $4,120 down to $4,001 in a single day. The subsequent recovery attempts have been laboured, with price stalling in the $4,060\u2013$4,080 band rather than pressing back toward the highs. The desk's bias sits firmly bearish against this backdrop.\n\nImmediately below current price, the $4,022 prior reaction zone becomes the first meaningful reference point. That level has attracted buyers on three prior occasions, and a clean break beneath it on a closing basis would represent a meaningful deterioration in near-term structure. Below that, the $3,957 zone \u2014 also a three-touch prior reaction area \u2014 represents the next logical area where demand could re-emerge.\n\nOn the hourly chart, the picture reinforces the bearish lean. Price spent most of Monday's session consolidating in the $4,087\u2013$4,105 band before rolling over through the Asian and early London hours on Tuesday. The H1 sequence shows a steady drift lower, with each hourly attempt to recover finding fresh offers. The $4,075\u2013$4,082 area has flipped from intraday support to what now looks like resistance on the shorter timeframe, and the most recent candles are pressing toward the $4,040 region.\n\nToday's calendar is empty of scheduled data releases, which removes one potential source of sharp directional volatility. In the absence of a macro catalyst, price action is likely to remain technically driven, meaning the zone interactions described above carry greater weight than they might on a high-impact data day. Thin conditions can, however, amplify moves in either direction.\n\nThe scenario that would challenge the current bearish read is a sustained recovery above the $4,082\u2013$4,100 intraday resistance band on a closing hourly basis, and more meaningfully, a daily close back above $4,130. That would suggest the recent pullback is corrective rather than the beginning of a more substantive retracement. Until that occurs, the structure continues to favour the downside.\n\nThis is editorial analysis, not financial advice. Trading involves substantial risk of loss.", "sample": false}